{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"dewy-rotatable-tarsier","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\nThe FIRST `initialize` handled by a freshly deployed mcp container takes far longer than the\n30-second default startup timeout most MCP clients use. Every client after it sees a warm process\nand connects instantly. So after each redeploy, the first client to arrive fails — and on a box\nwhose autodeploy ticks every 2 minutes, that recurs constantly.\n\n## Measured, against the deployed server\n\nThree consecutive `initialize` calls, identical payload, authenticated:\n\n    attempt 1   http=200   8.43s\n    attempt 2   http=200   0.26s\n    attempt 3   http=200   0.25s\n\nand immediately after a redeploy, an earlier attempt did not complete within a 60-second bound at\nall (`http=000`, 60.003s). The full startup sequence shows the cost is isolated to `initialize`:\n\n    initialize             http=000  60.00s   <- the whole problem\n    notifications/init     http=202  15.42s\n    tools/list             http=200   0.49s\n    prompts/list           http=200   0.25s\n    resources/list         http=200   0.25s\n\nEverything after the handshake is sub-second. This is lazy first-call work, not a slow server.\n\n## Observed client impact\n\n    MCP client for 'rebar' timed out after 30 seconds.\n    MCP startup incomplete (failed: rebar)\n\nReported repeatedly from Codex, whose default `startup_timeout_sec` is 30.\n\n## Likely contributor — and it may be MY doing\n\n`facial-lunar-urson` added `--extra bedrock` to the mcp image, which pulls in **boto3**. boto3\nis a notoriously heavy import (large botocore data files loaded at first use). If the provider is\nconstructed or imported during `initialize`, that fix plausibly made this WORSE. That is a\nhypothesis, not a finding — it should be measured, not assumed, and the fix should not be reverted\non suspicion.\n\nOther candidates worth timing before choosing one: building the 61-tool registry, the tracker\nstore's first read, and any config discovery that touches the filesystem or git.\n\n## Why P1\n\n- It is not self-healing FOR THE AFFECTED CLIENT. The client fails and stays failed; only a later\n  connection benefits from the warm process.\n- It recurs on every mcp deploy, and mcp deploys are frequent.\n- It makes the MCP surface look unreliable in exactly the first impression a user gets.\n\n## Corrects an earlier misdiagnosis, recorded so it is not repeated\n\nThis symptom was previously attributed to the blue-green CUTOVER orphaning sessions\n(`nemophilic-prettyish-cockroach`). That was wrong. The correlation with deploys was real, but\nthe mechanism is cold-start latency on `initialize`, not session loss. The discriminator is the\nthree-attempt sequence above: a cutover explanation predicts failure regardless of attempt order,\nwhereas the measured pattern is slow-then-fast, which is warming.\n\nThe evidence had also been misread once: raising `startup_timeout_sec` to 120 made it work, and\nthat was dismissed as 'the timeout value was irrelevant' on the basis that it then connected\n'immediately'. It was not irrelevant — it was the mitigation.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The dominant contributor to first-`initialize` latency is identified BY MEASUREMENT\n      (import timing / profiling), not by inspection.\n- [ ] First `initialize` on a cold container completes well within 30s, or the expensive work is\n      moved off the handshake (eager warm-up at container start, or made lazy per-tool).\n- [ ] A check fails if cold-start `initialize` regresses past an agreed budget.\n- [ ] LIVE VALIDATION — REQUIRED TO CLOSE: immediately after a redeploy, a first `initialize`\n      against `https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp` completes inside 30s, with the timing\n      recorded in a ticket comment.\n\n## Interim mitigation applied\n\n`~/.codex/config.toml` now sets `startup_timeout_sec = 120` with the measurements in a comment.\nThat is a client-side tolerance for a real server-side cost, and should not be treated as closing\nthis.","id":"d240-824a-dd04-4cdb","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["mcp","performance","startup","deploy"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"First MCP initialize after a redeploy exceeds 30s, so the first client to connect always fails"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787877799708022001,"uuid":"fdeda55f-200d-4902-9461-6cc03298ba73"}